# 37 - Deduplication Patterns (Real DE Practical)

This practical covers:
- dropDuplicates()
- dedup using window (latest record)
- dedup in incremental loads
- best practices

Deduplication is extremely common in pipelines.

---

## 0) Setup

```python
from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_timestamp, row_number
from pyspark.sql.window import Window
```

---

## 1) Sample Duplicate Data

```python
data = [
    (1, "Pramod", "2026-02-01 10:00:00"),
    (1, "Pramod", "2026-02-01 12:00:00"),  # duplicate with newer time
    (2, "Akash", "2026-02-01 09:00:00"),
    (2, "Akash", "2026-02-01 09:00:00"),  # exact duplicate
    (3, "Rahul", "2026-02-02 11:00:00"),
]

df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ["id", "name", "event_time"])
df = df.withColumn("event_time", to_timestamp(col("event_time")))
df.show(truncate=False)
```

---

## 2) Dedup using dropDuplicates()

### 2.1 Exact duplicates

```python
df.dropDuplicates().show(truncate=False)
```

---

### 2.2 Dedup by subset of columns

```python
df.dropDuplicates(["id"]).show(truncate=False)
```

⚠️ This keeps random row per id (not latest).

---

## 3) Dedup Using Window (Latest Record) (Most Important)

```python
w = Window.partitionBy("id").orderBy(col("event_time").desc())

df_latest = df.withColumn("rn", row_number().over(w))     .filter(col("rn") == 1)     .drop("rn")

df_latest.show(truncate=False)
```

---

## 4) Dedup Before MERGE (Incremental Load)

Real pattern:
1) source batch may have duplicates
2) dedup using window
3) then merge into target

---

## 5) Best Practices

✅ Use dropDuplicates for exact duplicates  
✅ Use window for latest record dedup  
✅ Always dedup before MERGE  
✅ Use watermark + event_time in streaming  

---

## 6) Summary

- dropDuplicates is simple.
- window dedup is production standard.
- latest record dedup is asked in interviews.

